(; constants used by Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Parity, declared with their translated types ;)

(; doc: An `AddMonoid` is an `AddSemigroup` with an element `0` such that `0 + a = a + 0 = a`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddMonoid ;)
AddMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An `AddMonoidWithOne` is an `AddMonoid` with a `1`. It also contains data for the unique homomorphism `ℕ → R`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddMonoidWithOne ;)
AddMonoidWithOne : (R : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Units of an `AddMonoid`, bundled version.  An element of an `AddMonoid` is a unit if it has a two-sided additive inverse. This version bundles the inverse element so that it can be computed. For a predicate see `isAddUnit`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddUnits ;)
AddUnits : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: The underlying value in the base `AddMonoid`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddUnits.val ;)
AddUnits__val : (self : Elem (AddUnits X)) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A `DivisionMonoid` is a `DivInvMonoid` with involutive inversion and such that `(a * b)⁻¹ = b⁻¹ * a⁻¹` and `a * b = 1 → a⁻¹ = b`.  This is the immediate common ancestor of `Group` and `GroupWithZero`. ;)
(; //LEAN:DivisionMonoid ;)
DivisionMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: **Alias** of `dvd_trans`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Dvd.dvd.trans ;)
Dvd__dvd__trans : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semigroup X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (divide a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (divide b c)) -> Proof (divide a c).

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mp : α → β` is the induced "cast" operation, mapping elements of `α` to elements of `β`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mp` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mp ;)
Eq__mp : (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem Y.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mpr : β → α` is the induced "cast" operation in the reverse direction, mapping elements of `β` to elements of `α`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mpr` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mpr ;)
Eq__mpr : (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `Eq.refl a : a = a` is reflexivity, the unique constructor of the equality type. See also `rfl`, which is usually used instead. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.refl ;)
Eq__refl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

(; doc: Equality is symmetric: if `a = b` then `b = a`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have a variable `h : a = b`, `h.symm` can be used as shorthand for `Eq.symm h` as a proof of `b = a`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.symm ;)
Eq__symm : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> Proof (Eqt X b a).

(; doc: Equality is transitive: if `a = b` and `b = c` then `a = c`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have variables or expressions `h₁ : a = b` and `h₂ : b = c`, you can use `h₁.trans h₂ : a = c` as shorthand for `Eq.trans h₁ h₂`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.trans ;)
Eq__trans : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt X b c)) -> Proof (Eqt X a c).

(; doc: A function is involutive, if `f ∘ f = id`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Involutive ;)
Function__Involutive : (f : (Elem X -> X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `a % b` computes the remainder upon dividing `a` by `b`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent. * For `Nat` and `Int` it satisfies `a % b + b * (a / b) = a`,   and `a % 0` is defined to be `a`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `%` in identifiers is `mod`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HMod.hMod ;)
HMod__hMod : (x1 : Elem X) -> (x2 : Elem Y) -> Elem x.

(; doc: Typeclass for a negation operator that distributes across multiplication.  This is useful for dealing with submonoids of a ring that contain `-1` without having to duplicate lemmas. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasDistribNeg ;)
HasDistribNeg : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Iff.rfl ;)
Iff__rfl : (a : Prop) -> Proof (iff a a).

(; doc: A mixin for cancellative addition. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsCancelAdd ;)
IsCancelAdd : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An element `a` of a type `α` with multiplication satisfies `IsSquare a` if `a = r * r`, for some root `r : α`.  [OEIS A000290](https://oeis.org/A000290) ;)
(; //LEAN:IsSquare ;)
IsSquare : (a : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: An element `a : M` of a `Monoid` is a unit if it has a two-sided inverse. The actual definition says that `a` is equal to some `u : Mˣ`, where `Mˣ` is a bundled version of `IsUnit`. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsUnit ;)
IsUnit : (a : Elem M) -> Prop.

(; doc: A typeclass stating that multiplication is left distributive over addition. ;)
(; //LEAN:LeftDistribClass ;)
LeftDistribClass : (R : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A `Monoid` is a `Semigroup` with an element `1` such that `1 * a = a * 1 = a`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Monoid ;)
Monoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Typeclass for expressing that a type `M₀` with multiplication and a zero satisfies `0 * a = 0` and `a * 0 = 0` for all `a : M₀`. ;)
(; //LEAN:MulZeroClass ;)
MulZeroClass : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Zero is a right absorbing element for multiplication ;)
(; //LEAN:MulZeroClass.mul_zero ;)
MulZeroClass__mul_zero : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulZeroClass X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X (times a 0) 0).

(; doc: `Nat.cast : ℕ → α` as a `RingHom` ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.castRingHom ;)
Nat__castRingHom : (X : Set) -> Elem (RingHom Nat X).

(; //LEAN:Nat.even_mul_succ_self ;)
Nat__even_mul_succ_self : (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (even (times n (plus n 1))).

(; doc: Iterate a function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.iterate ;)
Nat__iterate : (op : (Elem X -> X)) -> (x : Elem Nat) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `-a` computes the negative or opposite of `a`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `-` in identifiers is `neg` (when used as a unary operator). ;)
(; //LEAN:Neg.neg ;)
Neg__neg : (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A unital but not-necessarily-associative semiring. ;)
(; //LEAN:NonAssocSemiring ;)
NonAssocSemiring : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A typeclass stating that multiplication is right distributive over addition. ;)
(; //LEAN:RightDistribClass ;)
RightDistribClass : (R : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A `Ring` is a `Semiring` with negation making it an additive group. ;)
(; //LEAN:Ring ;)
Ring : (R : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Bundled semiring homomorphisms; use this for bundled ring homomorphisms too.  This extends from both `MonoidHom` and `MonoidWithZeroHom` in order to put the fields in a sensible order, even though `MonoidWithZeroHom` already extends `MonoidHom`. ;)
(; //LEAN:RingHom ;)
RingHom : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: A `Semiring` is a type with addition, multiplication, a `0` and a `1` where addition is commutative and associative, multiplication is associative and left and right distributive over addition, and `0` and `1` are additive and multiplicative identities. ;)
(; //LEAN:Semiring ;)
Semiring : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Turn a predicate `p : α → Prop` into a set, also written as `{x | p x}` ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.ofPred ;)
Set__ofPred : (Mem : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: Range of a function.  This function is more flexible than `f '' univ`, as the image requires that the domain is in Type and not an arbitrary Sort. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.range ;)
Set__range : (f : (Elem I -> X)) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: Units of a `Monoid`, bundled version. Notation: `αˣ`.  An element of a `Monoid` is a unit if it has a two-sided inverse. This version bundles the inverse element so that it can be computed. For a predicate see `IsUnit`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Units ;)
Units : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: The underlying value in the base `Monoid`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Units.val ;)
Units__val : (self : Elem (Units X)) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `Xor a b` is the exclusive-or of propositions. ;)
(; //LEAN:Xor ;)
Xor : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Xor.or ;)
Xor__or : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Xor a b)) -> Proof (or a b).

(; doc: Congruence in the function argument: if `a₁ = a₂` then `f a₁ = f a₂` for any (nondependent) function `f`. This is more powerful than it might look at first, because you can also use a lambda expression for `f` to prove that `<something containing a₁> = <something containing a₂>`. This function is used internally by tactics like `congr` and `simp` to apply equalities inside subterms.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congrArg ;)
congrArg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; //LEAN:eq_comm ;)
eq_comm : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Proof (iff (Eqt X a b) (Eqt X b a)).

(; //LEAN:exists_congr ;)
exists_congr : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (q : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (h : Proof (forall X (a2 => iff (p a2) (q a2)))) -> Proof (iff (exists X (a => p a)) (exists X (a1 => q a1))).

(; doc: The identity function. `id` takes an implicit argument `α : Sort u` (a type in any universe), and an argument `a : α`, and returns `a`.  Although this may look like a useless function, one application of the identity function is to explicitly put a type on an expression. If `e` has type `T`, and `T'` is definitionally equal to `T`, then `@id T' e` typechecks, and Lean knows that this expression has type `T'` rather than `T`. This can make a difference for typeclass inference, since `T` and `T'` may have different typeclass instances on them. `show T' from e` is sugar for an `@id T' e` expression. ;)
(; //LEAN:id ;)
id : (a : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `if c then t else e` is notation for `ite c t e`, "if-then-else", which decides to return `t` or `e` depending on whether `c` is true or false. The explicit argument `c : Prop` does not have any actual computational content, but there is an additional `[Decidable c]` argument synthesized by typeclass inference which actually determines how to evaluate `c` to true or false. Write `if h : c then t else e` instead for a "dependent if-then-else" `dite`, which allows `t`/`e` to use the fact that `c` is true/false.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `if c then t else e` in identifiers is `ite` (use `left` for `t` and `right` for `e`). ;)
(; //LEAN:ite ;)
ite : (c : Prop) -> (t : Elem X) -> (e : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:one_add_one_eq_two ;)
one_add_one_eq_two : (R : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddMonoidWithOne R)) -> Proof (Eqt R (plus 1 1) 2).

(; doc: The [axiom](https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.36.0-rc1/find/?domain=Verso.Genre.Manual.section&name=axioms) of **propositional extensionality**. It asserts that if propositions `a` and `b` are logically equivalent (that is, if `a` can be proved from `b` and vice versa), then `a` and `b` are *equal*, meaning `a` can be replaced with `b` in all contexts.  The standard logical connectives provably respect propositional extensionality. However, an axiom is needed for higher order expressions like `P a` where `P : Prop → Prop` is unknown, as well as for equality. Propositional extensionality is intuitionistically valid. ;)
(; //LEAN:propext ;)
propext : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (Eqt Prop a b).

(; doc: `rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the same as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.  This is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although the statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is definitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in Lean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality. ;)
(; //LEAN:rfl ;)
rfl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

(; //LEAN:two_mul ;)
two_mul : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NonAssocSemiring X)) -> (n : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X (times 2 n) (plus n n)).

(; //LEAN:zero_add ;)
zero_add : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddZeroClass M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (plus 0 a) a).

